        <section id="objective">
          <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">00</span>
            <h2>Objective</h2>
          </div>
          <p>By the end of this task, interns will understand how dark web marketplaces are <strong>structured</strong>,
            how <strong>legal monitoring tools</strong> like Ahmia.fi observe onion-space activity without ever touching
            Tor, and how to assess <strong>risk</strong> at both the marketplace level and the monitoring level. This is
            a defensive, analytical exercise for threat-intelligence, fraud-prevention, and AML-style roles.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="rules">
          <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span>
            <h2>Ground Rules</h2>
          </div>
          <p>No supervisor is watching in real time — these rules replace live oversight with fixed materials and a
            self-certification checklist.</p>
          <ul class="rules">
            <li><strong>No live dark web access.</strong> No Tor Browser installation, no visiting actual .onion sites —
              everything here works in a normal browser.</li>
            <li><strong>Fixed sources only.</strong> Use only the readings/queries specified in each subtask — no
              free-form searching for your own dark web material.</li>
            <li><strong>No click-throughs, ever.</strong> If a result looks illicit, record its title/category only — do
              not open it.</li>
            <li><strong>When unsure, stop.</strong> Log it as "flagged, not opened" and move to the next item.</li>
            <li><strong>Stay structural.</strong> Discuss how it works and how to detect it — never how to access, buy,
              sell, or replicate it.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="certline">REQUIRED IN FINAL REPORT → "I confirm I did not install Tor, visit any .onion address
            directly, or click through to any flagged result during this task."</div>
        </section>

        <section id="t1">
          <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span>
            <h2>The 5 Mini Subtasks</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="subtask" data-tag="reading only">
            <div class="subtask-head"><span class="subtask-index">1</span>
              <h3>Marketplace Structure Mapping</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="goal">Goal: understand how a dark web marketplace is typically organized.</p>
            <ul class="steps">
              <li>Read one official takedown press release from an official <code>.gov</code> or <code>.europa.eu</code>
                domain — search <code>"AlphaBay takedown DOJ"</code>, <code>"Hydra Market Europol"</code>, or
                <code>"Silk Road Ross Ulbricht DOJ press release"</code>.
              </li>
              <li>From the release, identify the roles mentioned: admins, vendors, buyers, moderators, escrow agents.
              </li>
              <li>Draw a simple org-chart-style diagram showing these roles and how they connect (e.g. buyer → escrow →
                vendor → dispute resolution).</li>
              <li>Add 1–2 sentences per role explaining its function, based on what you read.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="template-box">
              <div class="template-label">Sample Org-Chart Template — copy and fill in</div>
              <pre class="ascii">ADMIN
  │  (sets platform rules, takes commission)
  ▼
MODERATOR ── handles disputes, enforces rules
  │
  ▼
ESCROW AGENT ── holds payment until both sides confirm
  │
  ├──► VENDOR ── lists items/services, ships/delivers
  │
  └──► BUYER ── pays into escrow, confirms receipt

[Insert Diagram]  ← replace this line with your own chart (image, PowerPoint export, or hand-drawn scan)
[Insert 1–2 sentence role description for each box above]</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="deliverable"><b>Deliverable:</b> One diagram + short role descriptions.</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="t2">
          <div class="subtask" data-tag="public research">
            <div class="subtask-head"><span class="subtask-index">2</span>
              <h3>Payment &amp; Crypto Trace Case Study</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="goal">Goal: understand why certain cryptocurrencies are used, and how analytics firms trace them.
            </p>
            <ul class="steps">
              <li>Search Google Scholar for one paper: <code>"darknet market economics"</code> or
                <code>"cryptomarket trust reputation study"</code>. Read the abstract and any section on payments.
              </li>
              <li>Visit Chainalysis's public site and find their freely available Crypto Crime Report summary — no login
                required.</li>
              <li>Note which coins are commonly mentioned historically, and why (privacy features, liquidity,
                traceability).</li>
              <li>Summarize <strong>one real, publicly reported case</strong> where blockchain analytics helped
                de-anonymize transactions and led to a takedown. Use the curated list below if you want a
                beginner-friendly starting point.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="template-box">
              <div class="template-label">Curated Reading List — simplified case summaries (background only, not
                step-by-step)</div>
              <ul class="caselist">
                <li><b>Silk Road (2011–2013)</b> — one of the first major marketplaces, used Bitcoin exclusively. Shut
                  down by the FBI in 2013; investigators traced operational and financial mistakes by the site's founder
                  rather than breaking Bitcoin itself.</li>
                <li><b>AlphaBay (2014–2017)</b> — grew into one of the largest marketplaces before Silk Road's
                  successors. Taken down in a coordinated FBI/DEA/Europol operation ("Operation Bayonet") after
                  investigators linked an admin account to the founder's personal email.</li>
                <li><b>Hydra Market (2015–2022)</b> — a large Russian-language marketplace. Shut down by German federal
                  police in 2022, with U.S. Treasury sanctions following; blockchain analysis of its in-house exchange
                  helped map its financial flows.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="deliverable"><b>Deliverable:</b> Half-page write-up — "How crypto tracing helped unravel
              Marketplace X."</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="t3">
          <div class="subtask" data-tag="analysis">
            <div class="subtask-head"><span class="subtask-index">3</span>
              <h3>Risk Factor Matrix</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="goal">Goal: assess who is harmed by this economy and how organizations can respond.</p>
            <ul class="steps">
              <li>Based on Subtasks 1 &amp; 2, list 4–5 distinct risk categories (buyer/scam risk, AML exposure,
                societal harm, law-enforcement risk).</li>
              <li>For each, rate <strong>Likelihood</strong> (Low / Medium / High) and <strong>Impact</strong> (Low /
                Medium / High).</li>
              <li>For each risk, write one mitigation a legitimate organization (bank, exchange, enterprise) could
                realistically implement.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="template-box">
              <div class="template-label">Sample Table Format — copy and extend with 3–4 more rows</div>
              <table class="qtable">
                <tbody><tr>
                  <th>Risk Category</th>
                  <th>Likelihood</th>
                  <th>Impact</th>
                  <th>Mitigation</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Exit scam (vendor/admin disappears with escrow funds)</td>
                  <td>Medium</td>
                  <td>High</td>
                  <td>Multi-signature escrow, staged/partial payments, vendor bonding</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>[Insert Risk]</td>
                  <td>[Insert]</td>
                  <td>[Insert]</td>
                  <td>[Insert Mitigation]</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody></table>
            </div>
            <div class="deliverable"><b>Deliverable:</b> Likelihood × Impact table with mitigation notes (4–5 rows).
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="t4">
          <div class="subtask" data-tag="live · read-only">
            <div class="subtask-head"><span class="subtask-index">4</span>
              <h3>Safe Ahmia.fi Query Log</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="goal">Goal: observe what a legal, filtered onion-index search engine returns — using only fixed,
              pre-approved queries.</p>
            <ul class="steps">
              <li>Open a normal web browser (no Tor needed) and go to <code>ahmia.fi</code>.</li>
              <li>Results show only a title, the .onion address as plain text, and a short description — you will not
                click through to any of them.</li>
              <li>Run each of the 8 queries below, one at a time, in order, and log the results.</li>
              <li>If any result looks illicit, label its apparent category only (e.g. "appears to be marketplace-style
                listing") and move on — never click.</li>
            </ul>
            <table class="qtable">
              <tbody><tr>
                <th>#</th>
                <th>Query</th>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>news</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>2</td>
                <td>library</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>3</td>
                <td>whistleblower</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>4</td>
                <td>VPN</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>5</td>
                <td>forum</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>6</td>
                <td>human rights</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>7</td>
                <td>email service</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>8</td>
                <td>wiki</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody></table>
            <div class="deliverable"><b>Deliverable:</b> Completed 8-row query log (Query / # Results / Apparent
              Category / Notes).</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="t5">
          <div class="subtask" data-tag="synthesis">
            <div class="subtask-head"><span class="subtask-index">5</span>
              <h3>Categorization + Synthesis Report</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="goal">Goal: connect structure, payments, risk, and monitoring into one short analytical report.
            </p>
            <ul class="steps">
              <li>Group all Subtask 4 results into categories: legitimate tools, activism resources, forums, ambiguous,
                apparent illicit.</li>
              <li>Make a simple chart or bulleted breakdown of the proportion of each category.</li>
              <li>Write a 1–2 page synthesis: how structure relates to what's indexed, what signals suggest elevated
                risk, and why organizations monitor onion-space activity vs. the limits of a search-only approach.</li>
              <li>Add a 150–200 word reflection guided by these two questions:
                <ul class="subq">
                  <li>Which factor makes this economy most resilient?</li>
                  <li>Which disruption method is most effective?</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>Include the self-certification line from Section 01 at the top of this report.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="deliverable"><b>Deliverable:</b> Final synthesis report (1–2 pages) with self-certification,
              category chart, and reflection.</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="assess">
          <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">07</span>
            <h2>Assessment Criteria</h2>
          </div>
          <table class="data">
            <tbody><tr>
              <th>Criteria</th>
              <th>Weight</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Adherence to safety rules (fixed sources/queries only, no click-throughs)</td>
              <td>30%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Subtasks 1–3 analytical depth (structure, payments, risk)</td>
              <td>25%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Subtask 4 query log quality</td>
              <td>20%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Subtask 5 synthesis and reflection quality</td>
              <td>25%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody></table>
        </section>

        <section id="coord">
          <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">08</span>
            <h2>Program Coordinator Notes</h2>
          </div>
          <p>Asynchronous review — no live session required.</p>
          <ul class="notes">
            <li>Confirm the self-certification line is present, and that the Subtask 4 log only contains the 8 fixed
              queries — no free-form searches.</li>
            <li>If a submission mentions installing Tor, visiting a live .onion address, or clicking a flagged result,
              follow up directly before grading — this is a compliance issue, not a quality one.</li>
            <li>Frame feedback around "How would I detect or disrupt this?" — never "How would I participate in this?"
            </li>
            <li>Consider a short recorded video introducing commercial monitoring platforms (Chainalysis, Recorded
              Future, Flashpoint, DarkOwl) as the natural next step.</li>
            <li>If you encounter anything uncomfortable or confusing during Subtask 4, please raise a support ticket or
              send an email to unnati@digitalinvestigation.in.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="faq">
          <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">09</span>
            <h2>Quick FAQ</h2>
          </div>
          <p>Common doubts, answered here so you don't need to wait for a reply.</p>
          <div class="faq-list">
            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-q">Q: What if a search result looks unsafe?</div>
              <div class="faq-a">A: Don't click it. Just write down the title and your best-guess category (e.g.
                "possible marketplace listing") in your log, mark it as flagged, and move to the next query.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-q">Q: How do I log a flagged entry?</div>
              <div class="faq-a">A: In your Subtask 4 table, fill the "Notes" column with
                <code>FLAGGED — not opened</code> and a one-phrase reason (e.g. "title suggests illicit goods"). That's
                all that's needed — no further investigation.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-q">Q: Can I use a query that isn't on the list of 8?</div>
              <div class="faq-a">A: No. Stick to the fixed 8 queries in Subtask 4. Free-form queries aren't part of this
                task.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-q">Q: What if I accidentally click something I shouldn't have?</div>
              <div class="faq-a">A: Close the tab immediately, note it honestly in your report instead of the
                self-certification line, and message your coordinator. This is a process issue, not a punishment — being
                upfront is what matters.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-q">Q: Where do I ask something not covered here?</div>
              <div class="faq-a">A: You can raise a support ticket or email at unnati@digitalinvestigation.in.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

      </main>
